<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    THE KEY CAP. Read Shared.axaml's header first — it carries the authoring contract, and
    BaseButton carries the template shape, the type step, the focus ring and the disabled face.

    This is the densest state matrix in the app: five faces compete for one 26px square, four badges
    compete for its four corners, and the cap has neighbours 4px away that a ring must not eat. Four
    things are therefore settled here rather than left to the file's order:

      1. WHAT BEATS WHAT. Styles/Keyboard.axaml used to get its precedence from where a style sat in
         the file — `listening` won because it was written last. Every state below instead names the
         states it loses to, so the matrix survives a reorder. The order pinned is:

             listening  >  selected  >  :pointerover  >  locked  >  rest

         read as "listening's face wins over selected's, which wins over hover's, ...". Focus sits
         outside that ladder entirely: it owns the BORDER whenever it is visible and touches no
         face, which is why every state's BorderBrush setter carries `:not(:focus-visible)` and no
         state's Background setter does.

         THE LADDER IS THE LAYOUT BOARD'S, because it is the board the ladder's top two states are
         DRAWN on. `.selected` paints no face on a `.lighting` cap (#133, below), so every state
         that names `:not(.selected)` is answering a question that surface never asks. Two of them
         are restated for it and one is not:

           `.locked`'s face and border ARE restated (`^.locked.lighting…`, see LOCKED below).
           Losing them made a selected locked cap render differently from an unselected one, and
           the border half of that is visible: the cap's CONTENT covers everything inside the 1px
           border on that board, so Root's Background is hidden there and Root's BORDER is not.

           HOVER IS NOT. `^:pointerover:not(.selected)` leaves a selected cap at the resting
           surface rather than lifting it — a Background, on the board where no Background this
           theme sets is ever drawn. It is also somebody else's setter rather than part of
           locked's own statement, so buying an invisible case would cost an edit to a state that
           has nothing to do with this one.

         `.modified` LEFT that ladder with issue #91: a remap is a bar, not a face — see THE BADGE
         VOCABULARY below.

         `.paintSelected` IS NOT IN THE LADDER EITHER, and never will be: it paints no face at all.
         It beats nothing and loses to nothing except `:disabled`, which takes its ring the way it
         takes `.selected`'s — a dead cap wears no accent — and except the SURFACE: it is drawn
         only where `.lighting` is, because the selection it shows belongs to that board (#131; see
         PAINT-SELECTED below). It does not fight `.selected` (they own
         different elements and a cap may wear both), it does not fight the face states (the
         Lighting board's face is the key's own colour, drawn by the cap's content, not by this
         theme), and it does not fight focus, which owns the border as it always does.

         `.selected` IS SURFACE-SCOPED TOO, from the other side (#133). Everything the paragraph
         above says about the paint selection leaking onto the Layout board was equally true in
         reverse and stayed true one issue longer: the inspector's key kept its face, its accent
         border and its ring on the LIGHTING board, where the inspector is not even on screen. Its
         three setters therefore carry `:not(.lighting)`, exactly as the paint selection's carry
         `.lighting`. The two gates are one rule read in both directions — A SELECTION IS DRAWN ONLY
         ON THE BOARD THAT OWNS IT — and neither one clears anything: both classes stay written on
         every cap, so a trip to the other tab and back finds the selection where it was left.

         `:disabled` sits outside it from the other side and beats the whole ladder: the board is
         dimmed as a unit while a profile loads, so `.selected` and `.listening` carry
         `:not(:disabled)` and BaseButton's inset face is what a dead cap wears. `.locked` is the
         exception and needs no qualifier — its face IS the inset surface, so the two agree.

      2. SELECTION AND FOCUS AT THE SAME TIME (contract 4, and the acceptance criterion of the
         issue). Both are spread-only shadows in the accent family, so laying them on the same
         element would produce one denser ring and nothing legible. They are made CONCENTRIC:

             selection paints the outer 3px of the cap, INWARD from its edge;
             focus paints 3px OUTWARD from that same edge.

         A cap that is both wears a continuous 6px band with its own edge at the seam, and each
         half is still readable on its own. Nothing of the selection ring lands on a neighbouring
         cap, which is the constraint the design states outright ("it has to read on a 26px key
         without eating its neighbour").

         Mechanically that needs a second element: a Border has exactly one BoxShadow, BaseButton
         has already spoken for Root's with the focus halo, and two `{DynamicResource}` entries
         cannot be composed into one BoxShadows value in XAML. `Border#Ring` below is that element —
         inset so that its own spread lands inside the cap rather than outside it.

         THE SELECTION RING IS OPAQUE, AND TWO-TONE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF ISSUE #128. It used
         to be `ShadowKeyHalo` — the handoff's 2px of accent at 30% alpha — over a 1px accent
         border, which is legible on the Layout board's neutral cap and INVISIBLE on the Lighting
         board's. There the cap's face is an arbitrary saturated led colour drawn right up to the
         inside of the border by KeyCapView's fill panel, and 30% of blue over #A41010 is a shade
         of red. `.paintSelected` paints no face to fall back on, so the wash was the entire
         signal and the user could not tell a selected key from an unselected one.

         Alpha is the failure mode, so the answer is opacity and a contrasting second stroke, not
         more blue: `ShadowKeyHaloStrong` is 2px of the accent at FULL opacity with a 1px
         near-black hairline (`KeycapRingEdge`) inside it. One of the two always contrasts with
         whatever the key is lit — the accent carries a red, a green or a white face, the hairline
         carries a blue or an accent-coloured one — which no single colour can promise against
         device data. The handoff's own value stays declared and is deliberately unedited; this is
         a third token beside it, and the deviation is recorded in docs/app/design-system.md.

         THERE ARE TWO SELECTIONS, AND THEY ARE DIFFERENT SELECTIONS. `.selected` is the Layout
         board's ONE selected key — the one the inspector rail is talking about. `.paintSelected` is
         the Lighting board's paint selection, which is a MULTI-selection ("Paint · 2 keys
         selected", mockup 2f) and answers a different question. Both tabs render the very same cap
         view models, so both classes can be true on one cap at once — and each therefore owns its
         own element, `Border#Ring` and `Border#PaintRing`, rather than sharing one. Two states
         setting one BoxShadow is a matrix that works only while the two values agree; the moment
         either ring's geometry changes (as `.listening` already changes Ring's) the other would
         silently move with it.

         AND EACH IS DRAWN ONLY ON ITS OWN BOARD. Owning different elements keeps the two rings from
         merging; it does not stop either one appearing where it means nothing, which is what both
         of them did. `.selected` names `:not(.lighting)` and `.paintSelected` names `.lighting`, so
         the pair is complete: the inspector's ring on the Layout board, the paint ring on the
         Lighting board, and a cap wearing both classes wears exactly one ring on each picture.

      3. LOCKED IS A FACT, NOT A MOOD. "Hatching = not yours to edit" (docs/design/handoff.md,
         "Focus, selection, key badges"), so it is drawn as an overlay whose visibility depends on
         nothing but the class: a locked cap under the pointer, or selected, still reads as locked.
         The old matrix had hover repaint the locked face and lose the signal. Its face keeps
         following the ladder above; only the hatch and the dashed outline are unconditional.

      4. THE BADGE VOCABULARY — four marks, four corners, one hue each (handoff.md, "Focus,
         selection, key badges"): a bar across the bottom edge for a remap, a dot top-right for a
         macro, a right-triangle bottom-right for tap-and-hold, a rounded bar top-right for an
         advisory. Each is a PART of this template, because that is where a badge belongs: a style
         in Styles/ reaching in here is exactly what the control-theme layer exists to remove.

         WHY `.modified` IS NO LONGER A FACE. It used to paint a 14% accent fill, an accent border
         AND lift the caption to the accent — three accent signals for one state on a 26px cap, on
         top of the bar the design actually specifies. Mockups 1e/2a draw a remapped cap as an
         ORDINARY RAISED CAP WEARING ONLY THE BAR (2a, at true mock scale: `background: #EEF0F1;
         border: 1px solid #C9CFD2` with an absolutely-positioned 2px accent bar flush along the
         bottom). So the face, the border and the caption colour are gone and the bar is the signal.

         THE COLLISION THE DESIGN DOES NOT ANSWER. The macro dot and the advisory bar are both
         specified "top-right". Settled here: THE ADVISORY BAR KEEPS THE CORNER and the macro dot
         insets left of it by BadgeAdvisoryBarWidth + BadgeGap whenever both are true. Both stay
         visible, and the state that wants attention keeps the position the eye is trained on.

         A BADGE IS A FACT, LIKE THE HATCH. None of the four is qualified against `:disabled`, the
         ladder, or each other (beyond the collision above): "this key carries a macro" cannot stop
         being true because a pointer crossed it or because the board is loading.

         WHETHER TO DRAW THEM AT ALL is the PICTURE's answer, not the key's — `.stateBadges`, bound
         from KeyCapView.ShowsStateBadge. Same shape, and the same reason, as the lit face's
         `.lighting` / ShowsLighting: the Keys tab and the Lighting tab render the very same cap
         view models, so no state on the DataContext could separate them, and the Lighting tab
         wants to be about colour. The four classes still say what the KEY is; `.stateBadges` says
         whether this SURFACE draws it.
  -->

  <ControlTheme x:Key="KeyCapButton" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource BaseButton}">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaisedBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBorderRaisedBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusKeycap}" />

    <!-- The cap is drawn at whatever size the board's scale gives it (KeyboardPanel), so it must
         impose no minimum of its own, and its content — a caption over a colour strip — stretches
         into the whole face rather than centring as a button label would. -->
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="2" />
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />

    <!-- The design's keycap step, 9/400 (docs/design/handoff.md, "Typography"). It is quoted "at
         mock scale", and the board is now laid out at exactly that scale — a 1U cap is 30x26 — and
         scaled up as one unit by BoardScaleHost, so the type scales with the picture and 9 is the
         authored size rather than the drawn one. Set on the Button so everything the cap holds
         inherits it; the sub-legend steps down from here in Styles/Keyboard.axaml. -->
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeKeycapLabel}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Normal" />

    <Setter Property="Template">
      <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
        <!-- Contract 6, and it matters more here than anywhere else: the focus halo spreads 3px
             past Root into a grid whose caps are 4px apart, so a clip anywhere between the halo and
             the window erases it. False on the Button (BaseButton), on Root, on the stack and on
             Ring; KeyCapView sets it on itself for the same reason. -->
        <Border x:Name="Root"
                ClipToBounds="False"
                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}"
                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
          <Panel ClipToBounds="False">
            <!--
              LOCKED: the 45 degree hatch and the dashed outline, in one shape because they are one
              statement. A Border cannot dash (Avalonia 11 gives it no dash array), and a Rectangle
              can both fill and stroke, so the two halves of "hatched fill + dashed border" cost a
              single part.

              `Margin="-2"` cancels Root's own 2px padding so the hatch reaches the inside of the
              border instead of stopping at the caption's box; the two numbers are a pair and move
              together. RadiusX/Y are the chip radius (Themes/Geometry.axaml, RadiusChip = 3), one
              step inside the cap's own 4 — a CornerRadius resource cannot be handed to a Shape's
              double, so it is spelled out.

              The dash takes the hatch's own colour rather than the border role: locked is one
              material, and a second neutral at the edge would read as a border that happens to be
              broken. The locked state paints Root's border transparent to make room for it.
            -->
            <Rectangle x:Name="Locked"
                       Margin="-2"
                       IsVisible="False"
                       IsHitTestVisible="False"
                       RadiusX="3"
                       RadiusY="3"
                       Fill="{DynamicResource HatchBrush}"
                       Stroke="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}"
                       StrokeThickness="1"
                       StrokeDashArray="2,2" />

            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding ContentTemplate}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                              VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}"
                              RecognizesAccessKey="True" />

            <!--
              THE SELECTION RING. Empty, transparent, and present only to own a second BoxShadow.

              `Margin="0"` leaves its bounds exactly where the Panel's are, 3px inside the cap's
              outer edge (Root's 1px border plus its 2px padding). A spread-only shadow is drawn
              OUTSIDE the element it belongs to and clipped out beneath it, so from there a 3px
              spread paints exactly the cap's outer 3px and a 1px spread paints the third of those
              — which is how one BoxShadows value draws a 2px accent band with a hairline inside
              it, and why the two spreads are 3 and 1 rather than 2 and 1. Transparent rather than
              absent because a Border with no brush at all draws nothing, shadow included.

              The band grew from 2px to 3px with issue #128; it still runs INWARD, so the 4px gap
              between caps is as free as it ever was and the focus halo still owns it.

              `CornerRadius="1"` and not RadiusChip, because the radius is measured where the
              ELEMENT is: the cap's own curve is RadiusKeycap (4) at its edge, a shadow inflates
              the rounded rect by its spread, and 1 + 3 is the 4 the cap draws. RadiusChip's 3
              would have inflated to 6 and bulged the ring's corners past the cap's. It is spelled
              out for the reason `Rectangle#Locked` spells its own: a CornerRadius resource cannot
              be handed to a Border's shorthand and back out again.

              That margin is measured against a 1px border, so LISTENING — the one state that
              doubles the thickness — restates it; see the note beside that state.
            -->
            <Border x:Name="Ring"
                    Margin="0"
                    ClipToBounds="False"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False"
                    Background="Transparent"
                    CornerRadius="1" />

            <!--
              THE PAINT SELECTION'S RING — the Lighting board's own, and the second element of the
              pair described at the top of this file. Same geometry, same band, same two tones as
              `Border#Ring`: a selection is a selection, and drawing the paint one differently
              would say the two mean different amounts rather than that they answer different
              questions. It is also the ring the opaque two-tone was designed FOR — this is the
              one that sits on an led colour — and `.selected` takes it because the grammar is
              shared, not because the Layout board needed it.

              It is a SECOND Border and not a second setter on the first because both classes can
              be true on one cap: the Layout tab's selection and the Lighting tab's paint selection
              are computed on the SAME cap view models (KeyboardEditorViewModel.Apply hands its
              Layers straight to LightingTabViewModel.Attach), so nothing stops a key from being
              both the inspector's key and one of the painted ones. Sharing `Ring` would mean the
              two states writing one BoxShadow, which works only for as long as they agree — and
              `.listening` already writes a THIRD property onto both of these elements, which is
              exactly the kind of move that would drag one selection along behind the other.
            -->
            <Border x:Name="PaintRing"
                    Margin="0"
                    ClipToBounds="False"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False"
                    Background="Transparent"
                    CornerRadius="1" />

            <!--
              THE FOUR BADGES. Last in the Panel, so nothing — not the caption, not the hatch, not
              the selection ring's own band — is drawn over a mark that is the whole reason the cap
              is being looked at. None of them is hit-testable: the cap under them is one button.

              WHERE THE EDGES ARE, and why three of the four need no margin at all. The mockups
              inset a badge 2px from the INSIDE of the cap's 1px border, i.e. 3px from its outer
              edge. This Panel already sits exactly 3px in — Root's 1px border plus its 2px padding
              — so its own corners ARE the mockup's corners and an alignment is the whole placement.
              The remap bar is the exception: it is flush with the border rather than inset, so it
              carries `Margin="-2"` to cancel Root's padding, the same pair `Rectangle#Locked` uses.

              Sizes come from Themes/Geometry.axaml. The two numbers that cannot: a CornerRadius
              resource cannot be handed to a Border's per-corner shorthand, and the advisory bar's
              own radius has no token (RadiusChip's 3 on a 3px-tall bar would be a lozenge, and the
              mock draws 2). Both are spelled out below with the resource they shadow.
            -->

            <!-- REMAPPED — "you changed this", one of the accent's two sanctioned meanings. Flush
                 along the whole bottom edge, its lower corners following the cap's own (RadiusChip,
                 one step inside the cap's 4). This is the ENTIRE remap signal; see the header. -->
            <Border x:Name="RemapBar"
                    Margin="-2"
                    IsVisible="False"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False"
                    ClipToBounds="False"
                    VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                    Height="{StaticResource BadgeRemapBarHeight}"
                    CornerRadius="0,0,3,3"
                    Background="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />

            <!-- MACRO — the 5px dot, top-right. Steps left of the advisory bar when both are on;
                 see the collision rule at the foot of this file. -->
            <Ellipse x:Name="MacroDot"
                     IsVisible="False"
                     IsHitTestVisible="False"
                     HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                     VerticalAlignment="Top"
                     Width="{StaticResource BadgeMacroDotSize}"
                     Height="{StaticResource BadgeMacroDotSize}"
                     Fill="{DynamicResource BadgeMacroBrush}" />

            <!-- TAP-AND-HOLD — the ~6px right-triangle whose right angle is the cap's own
                 bottom-right corner, so the mark reads as the corner being folded rather than as
                 something sitting on the cap. Drawn at its authored coordinates (Stretch="None")
                 inside a box of exactly BadgeTapHoldSize, which is why the path's own 6s are
                 literals: a geometry is parsed from a string and cannot interpolate a resource,
                 the same constraint the hatch tile records. Keep the three in step. -->
            <Path x:Name="TapHoldMark"
                  IsVisible="False"
                  IsHitTestVisible="False"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                  VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                  Width="{StaticResource BadgeTapHoldSize}"
                  Height="{StaticResource BadgeTapHoldSize}"
                  Stretch="None"
                  Data="M 6,0 L 6,6 L 0,6 Z"
                  Fill="{DynamicResource BadgeTapHoldBrush}" />

            <!-- ADVISORY — the 12x3 rounded bar, top-right, in StatusAdvisoryStrong. Amber, never
                 red, and it never blocks (docs/design/README.md); it is the per-key half of the
                 "always shown twice" rule whose other half is the tab's one summary strip. It KEEPS
                 the corner when the macro dot is also on. -->
            <Border x:Name="AdvisoryBar"
                    IsVisible="False"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                    VerticalAlignment="Top"
                    Width="{StaticResource BadgeAdvisoryBarWidth}"
                    Height="{StaticResource BadgeAdvisoryBarHeight}"
                    CornerRadius="2"
                    Background="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryStrongBrush}" />
          </Panel>
        </Border>
      </ControlTemplate>
    </Setter>

    <!--
      MODIFIED sets nothing here on purpose. It has no face, no border and no caption colour: the
      2px accent bar at the bottom edge is the whole of the remap signal (see (4) in the header).
      `.modified` therefore does not appear in the ladder at all, and the state that used to sit at
      its bottom is `.locked`.

      `:not(:disabled)` is on the two states below for the reason Pills.axaml's header gives:
      Avalonia walks BasedOn first, so BaseButton's `:disabled` face is applied BEFORE anything
      declared here and an unqualified class would win over it. The board is disabled as a whole
      while a profile loads, and a cap that kept its accent fill and accent edge there would leave
      the picture looking live while nothing on it answered.
    -->

    <!--
      LOCKED — a position the firmware will not let anybody remap (specs/05-key-model.md §5.3): the
      SmartSet key, the Fn key. It sinks to the inset surface, so it reads as part of the board
      rather than as a cap raised out of it, and its border goes transparent because the dashed
      outline of `Rectangle#Locked` is now the edge.

      The hatch itself is unconditional — see (3) at the top. A locked cap is still a live button:
      clicking it selects it and the inspector explains the refusal, so it is never `:disabled` and
      never needs a qualifier against that state.

      `:not(.selected)` ON BOTH IS THE LADDER, AND IT IS THE LAYOUT BOARD'S LADDER. Locked loses its
      face and its edge to selection because a selected cap must read as selected — true where the
      selection is DRAWN, and false everywhere else, which is the whole of #133. The `.lighting`
      twins below restate both setters without that term, so on the board that draws no selection
      ring a locked cap is locked whether or not the Layout tab has picked it.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^.locked:not(:pointerover):not(.selected):not(.listening)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.locked:not(.selected):not(.listening):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.locked /template/ Rectangle#Locked">
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="True" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      ...and on a LIGHTING board the same statement is made with the outline alone. The Lighting
      board paints the key's colour over the cap's whole inner area, in the cap's CONTENT — which
      the template draws after `Rectangle#Locked` and before `Border#Ring` — so a lit locked cap
      would have its hatch and its dashed edge painted over and stop reading as locked at all.

      Two setters fix it and neither invents anything: the dash is lifted above the fill
      (`ZIndex`, which orders siblings inside this Panel and nothing else), and the hatch FILL is
      dropped, because on that board the whole face is already hatched under the colour — "off is
      hatched, never black" is the fill's own bottom layer. What is left is exactly the part the
      colour would otherwise have taken: the outline that says the position cannot be edited.

      `.lighting` is the picture's answer, pushed down from KeyCapView.ShowsLighting, the same
      shape as `.stateBadges`. The badges are off on that board, so nothing is hidden by the raised
      Rectangle; on any board that ever drew both, this would have to be revisited.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^.locked.lighting /template/ Rectangle#Locked">
      <Setter Property="ZIndex" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{x:Null}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      ...and the face and the edge are restated here WITHOUT `:not(.selected)`, which is the last
      corner of issue #133. Gating the three `.selected` setters stopped the selection being drawn
      on this board; it could not stop the selection being drawn by its ABSENCE. `.locked`'s two
      setters above yield to selection — correctly, on the board that shows one — so a locked cap
      the Layout tab had picked arrived here having quietly LOST its inset face and its transparent
      edge, and rendered differently from the identical cap beside it for a reason this picture
      cannot explain. A leak does not have to add a mark to be a leak.

      THE BORDER IS THE HALF THAT WAS VISIBLE. On this board the cap's CONTENT paints four fill
      layers over everything inside the 1px border — the same fact that lets the rule above drop the
      hatch's Fill — so Root's Background is covered and its BORDER is not. A selected locked cap
      therefore grew a solid neutral edge under its own dashed outline. The FACE is restated beside
      it because the two are one statement: "locked sinks into the board", and a pair that only half
      holds is exactly the drift the shared qualification exists to stop. It is also what makes the
      claim readable at the theme level, on a cap with no content to cover it.

      Nothing about the ladder changes: these two agree with the pair above wherever both match
      (same tokens, same values), so they can never fight and their order is not load-bearing. The
      only cap they answer differently for is the one the pair above abstains on — locked, lit and
      selected — and the answer they give it is "locked", which is what the Layout board's ladder
      would give it too if the selection were not being drawn there.

      WHAT IS NOT RESTATED IS HOVER, and the line is drawn there on purpose: `:pointerover` is a
      different STATE with a setter of its own, not part of locked's statement, so surface-scoping
      it would mean editing `^:pointerover:not(.selected)` rather than restating this pair. What it
      would buy is a hovered locked cap keeping the hover lift after the Layout tab selected it —
      a Background, on the board where no Background this theme sets is ever drawn. See the ladder
      note at the top of the file.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^.locked.lighting:not(:pointerover):not(.listening)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.locked.lighting:not(.listening):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      HOVER — the face lightens by one surface step and nothing else moves. It sets no border, so a
      locked cap keeps the dashed edge that identifies it while the pointer is over it, and it
      loses the face to selection and to listening.

      `:pressed` is deliberately absent. A cap's press is answered by the selection that follows it
      immediately, and Fluent's 75ms scale-down is gone with Fluent's template, so there is nothing
      to suppress and nothing to add.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^:pointerover:not(.selected):not(.listening)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceKeySelectedBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      SELECTED — the cap the inspector is talking about. Filled face, accent border and the opaque
      two-tone ring described at the top, drawn inward from the cap's edge.

      The ring is not qualified against `.listening`: the key that is listening is the key that is
      selected, and losing the ring at the moment the cap starts waiting for a keystroke would make
      it look like the selection had moved. The face and the border do yield to listening. It IS
      qualified against `:disabled`, like the face and the border: the ring is drawn in the accent,
      and a dead cap wears no accent.

      `:not(.lighting)` IS PART OF ALL THREE, and that is issue #133 — the exact mirror of what #131
      did to `.paintSelected`, left standing one issue longer because the leak points the other way.
      The Layout tab's selected key carries `IsSelected` on the LIGHTING board too, both tabs being
      the same cap view models, and there it drew a full accent ring, an accent border and a
      selected face over a picture whose whole subject is the key's own colour — on a board where
      the inspector rail is not even on screen (since #128 that column holds two IsVisible-gated
      children) and the selection therefore cannot be seen, explained or moved. The surface answers
      it, exactly as it answers the four badges and the lit face: the class still says what the KEY
      is and is written on both boards, and NOTHING IS CLEARED — leaving for the Lighting tab and
      coming back finds the same cap selected, the ring simply not drawn in between.

      The `.listening` margin setters below are deliberately NOT gated the same way; see the note
      beside them for why geometry and paint part company there.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^.selected:not(.lighting):not(:disabled):not(.listening)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceKeySelectedBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.selected:not(.lighting):not(:disabled):not(.listening):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.selected:not(.lighting):not(:disabled) /template/ Border#Ring">
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource ShadowKeyHaloStrong}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      PAINT-SELECTED — one of the caps the Lighting tab's paint actions will write to ("Paint · 2
      keys selected · Select all · Clear", mockup 2f). A different selection from `.selected`
      above: that one is the single key the inspector rail is talking about, this one is a set.

      It paints NO FACE, and cannot: on that board the face is the key's own colour. The inward
      ring is therefore the WHOLE signal rather than the loudest part of one, which is why issue
      #128 was reported here first and why the ring is opaque: a state with nothing to fall back on
      cannot be drawn in an alpha over a colour nobody chose. The grammar is the same one
      `.selected` uses, and the two are told apart by the SURFACE.

      `.lighting` IS PART OF THE STATE, and that is issue #131. This used to read `.paintSelected`
      alone, on the premise that the two selections "are never on screen together". THAT PREMISE IS
      FALSE, and falsely for the reason written three paragraphs up: both tabs render the same cap
      view models, so a key picked on the Lighting board carries `IsLightingSelected` on the LAYOUT
      board too — where it drew a full accent ring and border on a cap the user had never selected
      and could not deselect from there. The ring belongs to the board that owns the selection, so
      it is qualified by the picture's own answer, exactly as the four badges are qualified by
      `.stateBadges`. Nothing is cleared on a tab switch: the paint selection SURVIVES the trip and
      is simply not drawn where it means nothing.

      `:not(:disabled)` for the reason every accent state here carries it: BaseButton's disabled
      face is applied first, and an unqualified class would leave a dead cap wearing a live ring.
      `:not(:focus-visible)` on the border for the reason every other border setter carries it:
      focus owns the border whenever it is visible.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^.lighting.paintSelected:not(:disabled):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.lighting.paintSelected:not(:disabled) /template/ Border#PaintRing">
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource ShadowKeyHaloStrong}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      LISTENING — the cap is swallowing the next physical keystroke (specs/10-apps-and-ui.md,
      "Remap workflow"). Top of the ladder, because "press a key now" outranks every other thing the
      cap could be saying. Amber, since it is an advisory state rather than a success or a failure,
      and the border doubles to 2px so it is unmistakable at board scale.

      It also pulses, once per 1.4s. That animation lives on `Button.keyCap.listening` in
      Styles/Keyboard.axaml and animates the Button's own Opacity, not a template part, so replacing
      the template here leaves it untouched.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^.listening:not(:disabled)">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.listening:not(:disabled):not(:focus-visible)">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      ...and BOTH rings move with that thickness. Their `Margin="0"` is measured against a 1px
      border: the panel sits 3px inside the cap (1px border + 2px padding), which is where a 3px
      spread has to start for the band to reach the cap's edge. Doubling the border puts the panel
      4px in, so the same 0 would leave the band floating a pixel inside the edge instead of flush
      with it — visible on the one cap the eye is on, since a listening cap is by construction the
      selected cap and wears both. `-1` walks it back to 3.

      `Border#PaintRing` is restated as well, and that is not symmetry for its own sake: the
      Lighting board's caps and the Layout board's are the same view models, so a key can be
      listening and paint-selected at once, and a ring left behind by the thicker border would sit
      a pixel in on exactly the cap being pressed.

      Qualified exactly as the thickness above is, so the three can never drift apart — and
      deliberately NOT qualified by the SURFACE, unlike either ring's own shadow (`.lighting` on the
      paint ring's, `:not(.lighting)` on the inspector ring's): this is where the element sits, not
      whether it is painted, and geometry that only held on one board is the drift the shared
      qualification exists to prevent. Both elements are in the template on both boards whatever is
      drawn into them, so both keep the same bounds on both.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^.listening:not(:disabled) /template/ Border#Ring">
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="-1" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.listening:not(:disabled) /template/ Border#PaintRing">
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="-1" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      THE BADGES, switched on. Each is one class and one part, and every one of them is qualified by
      `.stateBadges` — the picture's answer to "are state badges on this surface at all?", pushed
      down from KeyCapView.ShowsStateBadge. The four state classes are still written whatever the
      surface is, because they say what the KEY is; only the drawing is the picture's call.

      Nothing else qualifies them. A badge is a fact, like the hatch: it does not yield to the face
      ladder, to the pointer or to `:disabled`.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^.stateBadges.modified /template/ Border#RemapBar">
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="True" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.stateBadges.macro /template/ Ellipse#MacroDot">
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="True" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.stateBadges.tapHold /template/ Path#TapHoldMark">
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="True" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^.stateBadges.advisory /template/ Border#AdvisoryBar">
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="True" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      ...and the one collision the design leaves open: the macro dot and the advisory bar are both
      specified top-right. The bar keeps the corner and the dot steps left of it, so a cap that is
      both still shows both and the state that wants attention stays where the eye looks for it.

      14 is BadgeAdvisoryBarWidth (12) + BadgeGap (2), spelled out because a Thickness cannot be
      composed from an x:Double in markup — the same constraint Themes/Geometry.axaml records for
      the dashboard grid's gap. Move either resource and this moves with it.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^.stateBadges.macro.advisory /template/ Ellipse#MacroDot">
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,14,0" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The focus ring itself comes from BaseButton and is not restated: accent on the border,
         ShadowFocusHalo on Border#Root. Every BorderBrush setter above yields to it. -->
  </ControlTheme>
</ResourceDictionary>
